* t58negoff.a -- CONF68K test t58: an F$STrap table placed AFTER its handler.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): the handler offset in an F$STrap initialisation* table is a SIGNED word, measured from the table entry, reaching plus or* minus 32K. So the routines may sit either side of the table.** Every other test here puts the table first, giving a positive offset. This* one puts it last, which is the ordinary shape when a module's tables live* at the end -- and is what a system reading the offset as unsigned gets* wrong, installing the handler 64K above the routine and killing the* process at the exception it meant to catch.** TRAPV is raised with the handler declared BELOW it in the source, so the* offset is negative. obs is 0 when the handler runs; a system that mis-reads* the offset cannot report at all -- the result line is MISSING, and* tools/conformance.sh names it.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1*FSTrap set $0ET_TRAPV set $1CVBIT set 2*EXPECT set 0* psect t58negoff,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart moveq #0,d0 movea.l d0,a0 lea exctbl(pc),a1 trap #0 dc.w FSTrap bcs.w setuperr ori #VBIT,ccr trapv moveq #8,d6 bra.w isfail*handler moveq #0,d6 cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.w ispass bra.w isfail** The table sits AFTER the handler: handler-*-4 is NEGATIVE here, which is* the whole point of the test.exctbl dc.w T_TRAPV,handler-*-4 dc.w -1* use verdict.i use report.i*idstr dc.b "RESULT t58 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  an F$STrap table may sit after the handler it names",0 ends